<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import InvoicesFrame from './lib/InvoicesFrame.abide'
import invoicesFrameSource from './lib/InvoicesFrame.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import listSource from './frame/index.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import invoicePageSource from './frame/invoice.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="The stylesheet is the switch">
        <Example
            id="view-transition"
            title="`view-transition-name` — a total that moves to where it lands"
            summary="Two invoices in a list, each total named after its invoice in the stylesheet. Open one: the total travels from its row to the head of the invoice, and back again from the link."
            files={{
                'frame/index.abide': listSource,
                'frame/invoice.abide': invoicePageSource,
                'lib/InvoicesFrame.abide': invoicesFrameSource,
            }}
        >
            <InvoicesFrame />
        </Example>

        <p>Naming an element is what makes the browser animate it from where it was on the outgoing
        page to where it is on the incoming one. A <code>::view-transition-old</code> or
        <code>::view-transition-new</code> selector does the same job for the whole document.</p>

        <p>Whether an app uses transitions at all is decided at <strong>build time</strong>, from
        the compiled <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> blocks. It is not a runtime walk of the document's
        stylesheets — that walk would run on every navigation of every app, including every app that
        has never wanted one.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The transition is awaited to the DOM write, not to the end">
        <p>The navigation waits for the point the <strong>DOM is written</strong>, and not for the
        animation to finish. That is the difference between a transition and a delay: an app whose
        transition is 400ms is not an app whose every navigation takes 400ms, and the values the
        incoming page loads are already in flight while it plays.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A browser without support navigates as it otherwise would">
        <p>There is nothing to feature-detect. A browser with no <code>view-transition-name</code>
        support renders the incoming page the way it would have with no rule at all, and an app is
        not asked to write the fallback — the fallback is the behavior the rule was an enhancement
        to.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/link-to-another-page')}>Links &amp; navigation</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/link-to-another-page')}>Links &amp; navigation</a> — what
            starts one of these</li>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/give-pages-the-same-chrome')}>Layouts</a> — the part of the
            document that does not transition</li>
            <li><a href={url('/templates/scope-styles-to-a-component')}>Styles</a> — where the rule
            is written</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
